⏺ The only code change needed was one file, vercel.json, which I've added.
  Everything else is dashboard settings.

  What the file does
  - Installs and builds from the repo root (npm ci, then npm run build -w
    frontend, output in frontend/dist). The frontend imports the shared
    workspace, so installing from the frontend folder alone would fail.
  - Sends every path to index.html, so a refresh or a shared link to /game/:id
    or a tournament page loads the app instead of Vercel's 404. Real files like
    the JS and CSS bundles are still served directly.

  Steps

  1. Commit and push vercel.json.
  2. Vercel → Add New → Project → import the repo.
     - Root Directory: leave blank. The file handles install, build and output,
       so leave the other build settings alone too.
     - Environment Variables:

  | Key                           | Value
                |
  |-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------
  ---------------|
  | VITE_API_URL                  | https://<your-service>.onrender.com, with no
  trailing path |
  | VITE_SUPABASE_URL             | same as your .env
                |
  | VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY | same as your .env
                |

     - Deploy. Note the production URL, e.g.
       https://othello-crosstie.vercel.app.
  3. Render: in render.yaml, uncomment the WEB_ORIGIN block with that Vercel URL
     (no trailing slash), then push. Without it, the browser blocks every API
     call from the Vercel site.
  4. Supabase → Authentication → URL Configuration: set Site URL to the Vercel
     URL and add it to Redirect URLs. Keep http://localhost:5173 there too so
     local development still works. Otherwise email confirmation links lead to
     localhost.

  Gotchas
  - VITE_* values are baked in at build time. If you change one in Vercel,
    redeploy for it to take effect.
  - Vercel preview deployments won't reach the API. Each preview gets its own
    URL, and WEB_ORIGIN only lists production. That's fine for now; WEB_ORIGIN
    takes a comma-separated list if you ever need a preview.
  - The Render free tier sleeps after about 15 minutes idle. The first page load
    after that waits about a minute for the API, so open the site once before a
    demo.

  To check it: open the Vercel URL in two browsers, sign in on one, and play a
  game against a guest on the other. A failure shows up as a CORS error in the
  browser console, which almost always means WEB_ORIGIN doesn't exactly match
  the Vercel URL.

